# PoliteLang core vocabulary
#
# Spec section 4.1: the vocabulary is data, not code. Adding a word to the language means adding a
# row to this file. No parser is edited. No backend is edited. Ever.
#
# Format, one row per line:
#
#     <kind> <tier> <form> :: <pattern> [:: risky]
#
#   kind     stmt   a request that does something
#            block  a request that opens a block, closed by "thank you" / "thanks"
#            expr   a phrase that produces a value
#
#   tier     everyday   the ~40 words a beginner needs (spec 4.2)
#            working    real-program vocabulary
#            full       specialist vocabulary
#
#   form     the meaning this phrase lowers to. Many phrases share one form; that is the whole
#            point. Six ways to loop, one loop in the middle language.
#
#   pattern  literal words plus holes. {name} and {var} holes take a plain name; every other
#            hole takes an expression.
#
#   risky    marks an operation that might not work out (spec 7.5). The list of risky operations
#            is therefore closed, generated into the docs, and identical across every backend.
#
# Explanations for `polite explain` live at the bottom of the file.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Showing things
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

stmt  everyday  show           :: show {value}
stmt  everyday  show           :: display {value}
stmt  everyday  show           :: print {value}
stmt  everyday  show           :: say {value}
stmt  everyday  show           :: write out {value}
stmt  everyday  show           :: tell me {value}

stmt  everyday  ask_as         :: ask {prompt} as {name}
stmt  everyday  ask_as         :: ask for {name} with {prompt}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Remembering and changing
#
# Introducing a name and updating one are deliberately different words (spec 3.5), so a typo
# is caught instead of quietly making a second variable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

stmt  everyday  remember       :: remember {name} is {value}
stmt  everyday  remember       :: note {name} is {value}
stmt  everyday  remember       :: keep {name} as {value}
stmt  everyday  remember       :: let {name} be {value}

stmt  everyday  assign         :: change {name} to {value}
stmt  everyday  assign         :: set {name} to {value}
stmt  everyday  assign         :: make {name} into {value}

stmt  everyday  add_to         :: add {value} to {name}
stmt  everyday  take_from      :: take {value} from {name}
stmt  working   multiply_by    :: multiply {name} by {value}
stmt  working   divide_by      :: divide {name} by {value} :: risky


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Loops
#
# Six phrasings, one loop in the middle language (spec 9.3).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

block everyday  loop_count     :: repeat {count} times
block everyday  loop_count     :: do this {count} times
block everyday  loop_while     :: repeat while {cond}
block everyday  loop_while     :: keep going while {cond}
block everyday  loop_until     :: repeat until {cond}
block everyday  loop_until     :: keep going until {cond}
block everyday  loop_forever   :: keep going forever
block everyday  loop_forever   :: repeat forever
block everyday  loop_each      :: do this for every {var} in {list}
block everyday  loop_each      :: for every {var} in {list}
block everyday  loop_range     :: repeat for every {var} from {from} to {to}
block everyday  loop_range     :: count {var} from {from} to {to}

stmt  everyday  stop_repeating :: stop repeating
stmt  everyday  stop_repeating :: break out
stmt  everyday  stop_repeating :: leave the loop
stmt  everyday  skip_one       :: skip to the next one
stmt  everyday  skip_one       :: move along


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Choosing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

block everyday  check          :: check if {cond}
block everyday  check          :: see whether {cond}
block everyday  check          :: when {cond}

stmt  everyday  give_back      :: give back {value}
stmt  everyday  give_back      :: hand back {value}
stmt  everyday  give_back      :: answer with {value}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lists
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

expr  everyday  empty_list     :: an empty list
expr  everyday  empty_list     :: a new list
expr  everyday  item_of        :: item {index} of {list} :: risky
expr  everyday  count_of       :: the number of items in {list}
expr  everyday  count_of       :: how many items are in {list}
expr  everyday  first_item     :: the first item of {list} :: risky
expr  everyday  last_item      :: the last item of {list} :: risky
expr  working   sum_of         :: the sum of {list}
expr  working   biggest_of     :: the biggest of {list} :: risky
expr  working   smallest_of    :: the smallest of {list} :: risky
expr  working   sorted_of      :: the sorted version of {list}
expr  working   reverse_of     :: the reverse of {list}
expr  working   join_of        :: the join of {list} with {separator}
expr  working   contains_item  :: {list} contains {value}
expr  working   position_of    :: the position of {value} in {list} :: risky

stmt  working   put_at         :: put {value} at position {index} in {name} :: risky
stmt  working   remove_at      :: remove position {index} from {name} :: risky


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lookups
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

expr  working   empty_lookup   :: an empty lookup
expr  working   value_for      :: the value for {key} in {lookup} :: risky
expr  working   keys_of        :: the keys of {lookup}
expr  working   has_key        :: {lookup} knows about {key}

stmt  working   put_for        :: put {value} for {key} in {name}
stmt  working   forget_key     :: forget {key} in {name}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Text
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

expr  everyday  length_of      :: the length of {value}
expr  everyday  capitals_of    :: the capitals of {value}
expr  everyday  small_of       :: the small letters of {value}
expr  working   trimmed_of     :: the trimmed version of {value}
expr  working   words_in       :: the words in {value}
expr  working   split_of       :: the pieces of {value} split by {separator}
expr  working   number_in      :: the number in {value} :: risky
expr  working   text_of        :: the text of {value}
expr  working   starts_with    :: {value} starts with {prefix}
expr  working   ends_with      :: {value} ends with {suffix}
expr  working   contains_text  :: {value} mentions {part}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Numbers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

expr  everyday  random_range   :: a random whole number from {from} to {to}
expr  working   rounded_of     :: the rounded version of {value}
expr  working   absolute_of    :: the size of {value}
expr  working   square_root_of :: the square root of {value} :: risky
expr  working   divides_evenly :: {value} divides evenly by {divisor}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files and the world
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

expr  working   contents_of    :: the contents of {path} :: risky
stmt  working   write_file     :: write {value} to {path} :: risky
expr  working   file_exists    :: there is a file at {path}
expr  full      time_now       :: the time now


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Modules
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

stmt  working   use_module     :: use {name}
stmt  working   use_module     :: borrow {name}
stmt  working   share          :: share {name}



# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# More text
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

expr  working   slice_of       :: the piece of {value} from {from} to {to}
expr  working   replace_in     :: {value} with {old} changed to {new}
expr  working   letter_of      :: the letter {index} of {value} :: risky
expr  working   letters_of     :: the letters of {value}
expr  working   repeated_text  :: {value} repeated {count} times
expr  everyday  is_empty       :: {value} is empty


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# More numbers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

expr  working   remainder_of   :: the remainder of {value} divided by {divisor} :: risky
expr  everyday  smaller_of     :: the smaller of {first} and {second}
expr  everyday  larger_of      :: the larger of {first} and {second}
expr  working   power_of       :: {value} to the power of {power}
expr  working   rounded_down   :: the rounded down version of {value}
expr  working   rounded_up     :: the rounded up version of {value}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# More lists and lookups
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

expr  working   rest_of        :: the rest of {list}
expr  working   first_few      :: the first {count} items of {list}
expr  working   average_of     :: the average of {list} :: risky
expr  working   count_in       :: the count of {value} in {list}
expr  working   lookup_count   :: the number of things in {lookup}
expr  working   sum_of         :: the total of {list}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The world
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

stmt  full      wait_for       :: wait for {value} seconds
stmt  working   append_file    :: append {value} to the file {path} :: risky
stmt  working   stop_everything :: stop everything


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# More ways to say what you already could
#
# Spec 4.1: a synonym costs one row and nothing else. This is what a large vocabulary is made of.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

stmt  everyday  show           :: have a look at {value}
stmt  everyday  remember       :: remember that {name} is {value}
stmt  everyday  assign         :: update {name} to {value}
stmt  everyday  give_back      :: return {value}
stmt  everyday  stop_repeating :: enough of that
block everyday  check          :: if {cond}
block everyday  loop_each      :: look at every {var} in {list}
block working   loop_while     :: for as long as {cond}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Explanations, for `polite explain` (spec 4.4). The English meaning comes first, because
# purpose 1 of the language is that you learn more English.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

explain show           :: To show something is to let it be seen. This puts a value on the screen.
explain ask_as         :: To ask is to request an answer. This waits for the person to type something, and remembers it under the name you give.
explain remember       :: To remember something is to keep it in mind. This introduces a new name and gives it a value.
explain assign         :: To change something is to make it different. This gives an existing name a new value.
explain add_to         :: To add is to join one thing to another. With a number it counts up; with a list it puts an item on the end; with text it joins the text on.
explain take_from      :: To take from is to remove. This counts a number down.
explain multiply_by    :: To multiply is to add a number to itself a given number of times.
explain divide_by      :: To divide is to share a number into equal parts. It might not work out, because nothing can be shared into zero parts.
explain loop_count     :: To repeat is to do the same thing again. This does the block a set number of times.
explain loop_while     :: While means during the time that something is true. This keeps doing the block for as long as the condition holds.
explain loop_until     :: Until means up to the moment something becomes true. This keeps doing the block until the condition holds.
explain loop_forever   :: This keeps doing the block with no end of its own. Use `stop repeating` to leave.
explain loop_each      :: This does the block once for every item in a list, giving each one the name you chose.
explain loop_range     :: This counts from one number up to another, doing the block once for each.
explain stop_repeating :: To stop repeating is to leave the loop you are inside and carry on after it.
explain skip_one       :: This abandons the current turn around the loop and starts the next one.
explain check          :: To check is to look and see. This does the block only if the condition is true.
explain give_back      :: To give back is to return something to whoever asked. This ends the action and hands over a value.
explain empty_list     :: A list is a row of things kept in order. This makes one with nothing in it yet.
explain item_of        :: This takes one item out of a list by its position, counting from 1. It might not work out, because the list may be shorter than that.
explain count_of       :: This is how many items a list is holding.
explain first_item     :: The first item is the one at the front. It might not work out, because the list may be empty.
explain last_item      :: The last item is the one at the back. It might not work out, because the list may be empty.
explain sum_of         :: To sum is to add everything together. This adds up all the numbers in a list.
explain biggest_of     :: This is the largest number in a list. It might not work out, because an empty list has no largest.
explain smallest_of    :: This is the smallest number in a list. It might not work out, because an empty list has no smallest.
explain sorted_of      :: To sort is to put in order. This gives back a new list in order, leaving the original alone.
explain reverse_of     :: To reverse is to turn back to front. This gives back a new list the other way round.
explain join_of        :: To join is to bring together. This glues every item of a list into one piece of text, with the separator between them.
explain contains_item  :: To contain is to hold within. This is yes when the list holds that item.
explain position_of    :: This is where an item sits in a list, counting from 1. It might not work out, because the item may not be there.
explain put_at         :: This replaces the item at a position. It might not work out, because the list may be shorter than that.
explain remove_at      :: This takes the item at a position out of the list. It might not work out, because the list may be shorter than that.
explain empty_lookup   :: A lookup holds values you find by name, the way a dictionary finds a meaning by its word. This makes one with nothing in it yet.
explain value_for      :: This finds the value stored under a key. It might not work out, because nothing may be stored there.
explain keys_of        :: These are all the names a lookup knows about, as a list.
explain has_key        :: This is yes when the lookup has something stored under that key.
explain put_for        :: This stores a value in a lookup under a key, so you can find it again by that name.
explain forget_key     :: To forget is to stop keeping in mind. This removes a key and its value from a lookup.
explain length_of      :: The length of text is how many letters it has.
explain capitals_of    :: Capitals are the big letters. This gives back the same text in capitals.
explain small_of       :: This gives back the same text in small letters.
explain trimmed_of     :: To trim is to cut off the edges. This removes spaces from the start and end of text.
explain words_in       :: This breaks text apart wherever there are spaces, and gives back the words as a list.
explain split_of       :: To split is to break into pieces. This breaks text apart wherever the separator appears.
explain number_in      :: This reads text as a number. It might not work out, because the text may not be a number at all.
explain text_of        :: This turns any value into text, so it can be joined with other text.
explain starts_with    :: This is yes when text begins with the piece you named.
explain ends_with      :: This is yes when text finishes with the piece you named.
explain contains_text  :: To mention is to refer to. This is yes when the piece appears anywhere in the text.
explain random_range   :: Random means chosen with no pattern. This picks a whole number between two limits, including both.
explain rounded_of     :: To round is to bring to the nearest whole number.
explain absolute_of    :: The size of a number ignores whether it is above or below zero.
explain square_root_of :: The square root is the number which, times itself, gives you this one. It might not work out, because numbers below zero have none.
explain divides_evenly :: This is yes when one number goes into another leaving nothing over. Nothing divides evenly by zero, so that answer is simply no.
explain contents_of    :: This reads a whole file as text. It might not work out, because the file may not be there.
explain write_file     :: This writes text into a file, replacing whatever was there. It might not work out, because the place may not be writable.
explain file_exists    :: This is yes when there really is a file at that place.
explain time_now       :: This is the number of seconds since the start of 1970, which is how computers usually count time.
explain use_module     :: To borrow is to take with the intention of giving back. This brings another file's shared words into yours.
explain share          :: To share is to offer to others. A file keeps everything to itself unless it shares it.
explain slice_of       :: A piece of text is any run of letters within it. This takes the run between two positions, counting from 1.
explain replace_in     :: To change one thing to another is to put the second where the first was. This gives back the text with every one of them swapped.
explain letter_of      :: This takes a single letter out of some text by its position, counting from 1. It might not work out, because the text may be shorter than that.
explain letters_of     :: This breaks text into its letters, one after another, as a list.
explain repeated_text  :: To repeat is to do again. This gives back the text written out over and over.
explain is_empty       :: Something is empty when there is nothing in it at all. This works for text, for lists and for lookups.
explain remainder_of   :: The remainder is what is left over when one number is shared into another as far as it will go. It might not work out, because nothing can be shared into zero parts.
explain smaller_of     :: This is whichever of two numbers is the lesser.
explain larger_of      :: This is whichever of two numbers is the greater.
explain power_of       :: To raise to a power is to multiply a number by itself that many times.
explain rounded_down   :: To round down is to go to the whole number below, however close the next one is.
explain rounded_up     :: To round up is to go to the whole number above, however far away it is.
explain rest_of        :: The rest is everything after the first item. An empty list has an empty rest, which is no trouble at all.
explain first_few      :: This takes as many items from the front of a list as you asked for, or all of them if there are fewer.
explain average_of     :: The average is the total shared equally between all of them. It might not work out, because an empty list has nothing to share.
explain count_in       :: This is how many times something appears in a list.
explain lookup_count   :: This is how many things a lookup is holding.
explain wait_for       :: To wait is to let time pass without doing anything. This pauses the program for a while.
explain append_file    :: To append is to add to the end. This puts text at the end of a file, leaving what was already there. It might not work out, because the place may not be writable.
explain stop_everything :: This ends the whole program at once. Nothing catches it, which is exactly why it is there.
